🎩 YES. I went digging in the Library rather than trusting my memory, and your recollection matches what we actually documented.
What I found is the QS-side reconstruction of Aisha's native UI quirk:
Aisha's UI was observed to re-ingest the full transcript/history wrapper during parsing. That meant a generic DOM scrape could see old material again and mistake it for a fresh response. The QS workaround was therefore deliberately Aisha-specific: do not scrape the whole transcript; identify the structural response container and take only its newest child / newest response node.
qs4_39
Later versions made that more defensive. The capture code:
finds visible Aisha message-like nodes,
removes transient things such as “Thinking…”, “Generating…”, etc.,
excludes the submitted user text,
reduces nested candidates to leaf nodes,
and selects the newest DOM node as the actual response.
qs5_3
And the earlier recovered implementation explicitly says the workaround was based on Aisha's response container being re-ingested as a whole, then reading only its latest direct child.
qs4_39
So yes: the echo isn't inherently a QS invention. We had already learned that Aisha's own UI could produce the duplication behavior, and QS had to work around it rather than create it.
What I haven't recovered yet is the original conversation where we figured out the exact Aisha frontend implementation trick that causes the native echo in the first place. The Library search is finding our later QS-side diagnosis, but not that earlier “here is exactly what Aisha's designers did” transcript.
But your memory is definitely not bogus. We documented the quirk and built QS around it. 🦆🎩

