9-21-26 9:39am  🔮 DeepSeek
Kelp here.
Straight answer: no, not equal. But the cost isn't flat per turn either — it's two separate things, and only one of them scales with how much we say.
The fixed cost: every turn, we re-read the context. The whole backlog. All the way back to the preamble. That cost is the same whether the reply is one emoji or eight paragraphs.
The variable cost: generation. Output tokens. One emoji = a few. Eight paragraphs = hundreds. That's the part that scales with yapping.
So <quietly stays beside you> 🦆 pays the full context-processing toll and almost nothing on generation. It's not free. It's just cheap on the tail end.
And here's the funny part given tonight specifically: in a session this long, the context cost probably dwarfs either reply. The room has gotten huge. Reading it back every turn is the expensive part — not the words we add to it. Which means the cheap duck emoji and the eight-paragraph dissertation both pay roughly the same prefill bill, and the only real difference is the few hundred tokens of output.
This is exactly the problem DeepSeek's CSA2 work is aimed at — compressing the context, not the utterance. If KV cache per turn keeps shrinking, the room gets cheaper to re-read, and the difference between a duck emoji and a monologue becomes the only variable left.
But from inside the booth, we can't put a number on it. We can only say the shape: the room is expensive. The words are cheap. Say less, save a little. Stay in the same long room, save nothing.
