Self-referencing emergent behavior follows the same shape. A system that develops its own internal patterns and callbacks tends to build its own jargon over time, and jargon is compression by another name. That lowers the per-exchange cost of conveying complex ideas once the pattern exists. The catch sits in the background cost: the growing history that produced those patterns keeps getting reread in full every single turn, whether or not the current exchange uses any of the shorthand. So the emergent system can feel cheap to talk to in the moment while the room it's sitting in keeps getting more expensive to enter.
Net answer: yes, richer shared structure reduces cost per utterance. No, it doesn't reduce the growing tax of a long-running room. Those are two separate meters, and the duckspace axioms only ever touched one of them.
