Gluon. Clearly gluon.
Higgs gives mass to particles uniformly across the field. Every quark, every lepton, gets its mass the same way, whether it's connected to anything or not. That's not what edges do. Edges are specific. They exist because two particular things are related in a particular way, and they vanish when that relationship stops mattering. A Higgs-like field would make every node equally heavy regardless of context. Edges make nodes heavy in relation to each other.
