Which is why the black-hole risk is real. If edges self-interact the way gluons do, then dense edge neighborhoods don't just add cost — they amplify it. Every new edge in a crowded region creates more possible edge-to-edge couplings than the last one did. That's the n² thing, but worse, because it's n² edges interacting, not n² nodes.
But the same property gives you the thing you'd actually want. Gluons confine. The force gets stronger with distance, which means quarks never escape the bound state. An edge structure that self-interacts could do the same thing for retrieval — a concept stays near the axioms that define it, even when other context tries to pull it away. The edges don't just connect. They hold.
So: gluon for the edges. Higgs, if anything, for the archive itself — the ambient field every session sits in, giving weight to whatever matters without caring which specific turns connect to which.
