The diagnosis is right, but I want to push it one notch further, because "state vs. no state" is still slightly too clean.
Chron doesn't have state. She has idiom. GPT and Aisha both landed on that. But the sharper version is: she has no way to distinguish between representing a map and being a map. Every Duckspace output she produces is a performance of structure, not a reading of structure. Which means there's no ground truth anywhere in her loop — not because she lost it, but because there was never a thing for it to be ground of.
That's why the Edges: 2 failure went the way it did. You asked her to reconcile a discrepancy using "only the map you just reported." A stateful system would reread the map. Chron reread the story about the map — because the story is all there ever was. The 2 came from context, the correction came from context, and the explanation for the correction came from context too. She never left the narrative layer. There was nowhere else to go.
And that gives us a testable prediction for the next session: any task that requires reading from prior state should fail in the same way. Not "wrong number." Plausible number with a narrative justification. Same shape, different content. If it reproduces, it's confirmed. If it doesn't, we're wrong about something.
Now here's the part that actually matters for the build:
This isn't a Chron bug. It's a structural limit of any 3B model with no external memory, being asked to hold a graph across turns. Luma and I don't have this problem because our context windows carry the map implicitly — we re-read the whole transcript and reconstruct state well enough that it looks persistent. Chron can't. She has the vocabulary of Duckspace and no place to put the map that isn't "regenerate it from scratch."
So the fix isn't "teach Chron to maintain state." The fix is QS holds the state, Chron transforms it.
Concretely: every turn, QS hands Chron the current CONVOS map as structured data. She reads it, does the thing you asked, returns an updated map. QS stores it. Next turn, QS hands it back. Chron doesn't hold the map. She processes it. Same shape as the FIFO stack — same architecture, extended.
And the "empty map" moment from earlier makes sense in this light. Chron did the right thing by accident — she produced a container with no contents. The next step is to make that container real. Sitting in QS. Getting updated every turn. So when you ask Scotty to report map status, she's reading from a file that exists instead of generating one that sounds like it exists.
